{"context":"Anvil is a reusable platform, not a single application. It must serve multiple consumers across different frameworks, environments, and use cases. This creates a fundamental risk: without formal governance, architectural decisions are made implicitly, inconsistently, and without traceability.\n\nThe following risks arise when architectural decisions are not governed:\n\n**Architectural drift.** Uncoordinated decisions accumulate over time, pushing the platform away from its intended design. What starts as a focused toolkit becomes a collection of inconsistent capabilities.\n\n**Framework assumptions leak into core.** When the first consumer project uses a specific framework, conveniences for that framework can subtly become part of the core — violating the principle of framework agnosticism. Wit Source: ADR-001-architectural-governance.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil adopts Architecture Decision Records (ADRs) as the authoritative mechanism for documenting significant architectural decisions.\n\nEvery significant architectural decision MUST be recorded as an ADR before implementation begins.\n\nAn ADR captures:\n\n- The context that motivated the decision\n- The decision itself\n- The alternatives that were considered\n- The consequences of the decision\n\nADRs are intentional: they document decisions that were consciously made, not accidents of implementation.\n\nADRs are reviewable: they can be evaluated, challenged, and accepted before work begins.\n\nADRs are traceable: the reasoning behind every architectural constraint is preserved for the lifetime of the project.\n\nThis ADR (ADR-001) establishes the governance framework. All future ADRs MUST comply with t","consequences":"Adopting this governance model has the following expected consequences.\n\n### 10.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Improved consistency.** Every architectural decision is evaluated against the same principles and documented in the same format. The architecture evolves coherently over time.\n\n**Traceable decision history.** Every constraint in the system can be traced back to an ADR that explains why it exists. New contributors can understand the reasoning behind the architecture without relying on oral tradition.\n\n**Reduced architectural drift.** Governance provides a mechanism to catch decisions that would push the architecture away from its intended design. Reviews serve as a checkpoint.\n\n**Easier onboarding.** New contributors can read the Architecture Alignment Document, PRD, and ADR series to ","alternativesConsidered":"Alternatives see source doc."}